const timeCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Deep time was the revolution', note: 'Hutton (1788) and Lyell (1830): the same slow processes we see today, operating over unimaginable durations — "the present is the key to the past". Geology invented deep time; Darwin needed it for evolution.' },
    { math: '2 · Relative dating first', note: 'Superposition (lower = older), cross-cutting (the cut is younger than the cut), fossils in predictable order (faunal succession). Geologists ordered all the layers before knowing any dates.' },
    { math: '3 · Radiometric clocks (1907→)', note: 'Radioactive decay at fixed rates: U–Pb in zircon (the champion — 4.4-billion-year grains dated), K–Ar, carbon-14 (organic material to ~50 kya — Nobel 1960). Earth: 4.54 ± 0.05 billion years (Patterson, 1956).' },
    { math: '4 · The timescale, honestly learned', note: 'Eons (Hadean→Archean→Proterozoic→Phanerozoic), then eras, periods, epochs — boundaries mark real events (the Chicxulub impact ends the Cretaceous). Dates are measured; the names are conventional.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/fossils-and-geologic-time">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Geologic time is <strong>the deepest idea in science: 4.54 billion years, measured</strong>.
    The logic built it in two steps — ordering by relationships (which layer is older?) and then
    dating by physics (exactly how old?). Fossils supplied the first correlation system; isotopes
    supplied the numbers.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="deep time, relative ordering, radiometric clocks, the timescale" widget="equationStep" config={timeCfg} />

  <h2>Fossils as instruments</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Preservation is rare and biased:</strong> hard parts, rapid burial, low oxygen — the fossil record samples life unevenly (soft-bodied organisms underrepresented; the Cambrian explosion partly an artefact of shells appearing — the debate continues with new chemistry).</li>
    <li><strong>Index fossils correlate globally:</strong> a species with wide range and short tenure dates the rock it is in — the 19th-century global timescale was built entirely this way, before any absolute dates existed.</li>
    <li><strong>Extinctions read from the record:</strong> five mass extinctions (end-Ordovician, Late Devonian, end-Permian "the Great Dying" ~96% of species, end-Triassic, end-Cretaceous) — each reset evolution\'s course; the sixth (current, human-driven) is measured against this baseline (the biology ladder\'s biodiversity lesson).</li>
    <li><strong>Patterson\'s meteorites:</strong> Earth rocks recycle (the rock-cycle lesson); meteorites do not — dating them dated the solar system, and incidentally revealed lead pollution in the modern world (the geologist-turned-public-health story).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hutton (1788); Lyell (1830); Patterson, Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta (1956); the International Chronostratigraphic Chart.</p>
</TopicPage>
